# Normalize all text files to LF in the repo, auto-convert on checkout
* text=auto eol=lf

# Explicitly mark binary files so Git never tries to diff/merge them
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.svg binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf binary
*.eot binary
*.mp4 binary
*.webm binary
*.pdf binary

# ── Append-only registries ──────────────────────────────────────────────
# apps/web/harness/README.md carries a table of harness routes that every
# feature branch appends a row to, at the same anchor. On 2026-09-02 that
# one file conflicted on 13 of 17 open branches, always the same way: two
# sides adding different rows to the same table. `union` keeps both sides'
# lines instead of writing markers, which is the right answer for a list
# whose entries are independent.
#
# The tradeoff is real and small: if two sides edit the same *prose*
# sentence here, union keeps both versions rather than flagging it. That
# is visible in review, where a conflict marker would be too, and it is
# worth trading for thirteen resolutions of a route table.
apps/web/harness/README.md merge=union

# -- pg_get_functiondef captures are evidence, not source -----------------
# MIGRATION_SAFETY_GUIDE Rule 1 requires the capture beside a
# CREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION migration to be the BYTE-EXACT output of
# pg_get_functiondef against the live database, and the apply protocol
# verifies it by comparing md5(file) against md5(pg_get_functiondef(...)).
#
# `* text=auto eol=lf` above rewrites line endings on checkout, which
# silently breaks that comparison. It is not theoretical: on 2026-09-03
# the live bodies of process_all_faab_waivers() and
# should_process_waivers_now() were found to contain CRLF (a Windows psql
# apply, Rule 3's own failure mode), git warned "CRLF will be replaced by
# LF the next time Git touches it" on the commit carrying their captures,
# and the next checkout would have made both captures fail to match a
# production they still describe correctly.
#
# `-text` disables all line-ending conversion for this directory, so the
# bytes committed are the bytes pg produced. Captures are append-only and
# never edited (see captures/README.md), so there is no merge cost.
supabase/migrations/captures/** -text
